# Git & commits

## Workflow
- Branch per work package: `wp1-ingestion`, `wp3-soiling-model`, etc.
- Small, focused commits. One logical change per commit. Never commit data files
  in `data/raw/` or `data/processed/` (see `.gitignore`); commit code, config,
  docs and small reference figures only.
- Open a PR into `main` per work package; do not push broken code to `main`.

## Commit message format
Conventional-commit prefix, imperative mood, written by a human professional:

    <type>(<scope>): <short summary, <= 72 chars>

    <body: what changed and WHY, wrapped at 72 cols. Reference the work
    package and any assumption or limitation a reviewer should know.>

Types: feat, fix, refactor, data, docs, test, chore, perf, build.

## Hard rules
- English only. Professional, plain engineering prose.
- No emoji. No AI-author phrasing. No "Generated with" or "Co-Authored-By: ...AI"
  trailers of any kind.
- Do not pad messages. If the change is one line, the message is one line.

## Good examples
    feat(ingestion): parse irradiance workbook into typed daily frame

    Reads the Canakkale irradiance/production workbook, normalizes the Turkish
    headers, coerces TARIH to datetime and validates a complete 2023-01-01..
    2025-10-22 daily index. WP1.

    fix(cleaning): drop curtailment days before soiling slope fit

    Curtailed days understate PI and biased the soiling rate. Excluded all days
    overlapping curtailment/downtime entries; logged 41 dropped rows. WP2.
